I think you just made my point for me.
Yes, you *can* do that sort of thing in C++, but it's horribly verbose and
rarely actually useful; most of the time you'll end up writing a for
loop, which exposes you to fencepost errors and other such humoristic
bugs.

The fact of the matter is, the only way to write for_each in a useful
manner is to use either anonymous functions or macros. Anything else is
too much typing, when the point of the exercise is to avoid that typing.

Python supports the former, in a crippled way.
C/C++ *kinda* supports macros, but not nearly as well as Python supports
lambda; you have to be excessively careful to avoid causing compilation,
or (worse) logic errors. In any case, one reasonable use of Lisp macros
is to run stuff at compile-time.

C++ arguably has template metaprogramming for that. I once won a book on
how to do it. "The horror! The undescribable horror!" doesn't even start.

